Ok, so first, a bug: Not all reminder text functions are evaluated. Example:
"Permanents you don't control that are dealt damage by this {if has_pt() then "creature"} are exiled."

Next, this is phase 1.6. Let's start with some more leftover features from previous phases:

- **Planeswalker parsing** (added 2026-04-30). The Planeswalker frame uses a different shape: loyalty cost lines (`+1:`, `-X:`, `0:`), starting loyalty, ability tiers — none of which the Phase 1 parser recognises. Need a planeswalker-specific face extractor + a place to store the loyalty bands.
- **Emblem parsing** (added 2026-04-30). Emblems are a thinner Planeswalker output (just rule_text, no cost / P/T / loyalty); they may be modelled as Tokens with a special design_type or a separate `emblems` table. Decide before writing the parser.
- **Saga parsing** (added 2026-04-30). Sagas have ordered chapters (`I`, `II`, `III`, …) with their own rule_text bodies and an "after the last chapter, sacrifice" rule. Needs a chapter list on the card record.
- **Leyline parsing** (added 2026-04-30). Leylines aren't structurally novel but they trip the design-type rules: most are on `m15-altered-beyond` with a self-replacing-from-hand ability that may want a tagged Design Type or a special note for the player view.

1. Planeswalker parsing

A planeswalker input is like this in the input file:

card:
	stylesheet: m15-mainframe-planeswalker
	stylesheet_version: 2024-09-01
	has_styling: false
	notes: 
	time_created: 2024-03-01 04:41:03
	time_modified: 2026-03-28 03:11:30
	name: Kathleen, Frog Queen
	casting_cost: 2WU
	image: 
	image_2: 
	mainframe_image: 
	mainframe_image_2: 
	super_type: <word-list-type-en>Legendary Planeswalker</word-list-type-en>
	sub_type: <word-list-planeswalker>Kathleen</word-list-planeswalker><soft><atom-sep> </atom-sep></soft><word-list-planeswalker></word-list-planeswalker>
	rarity: common
	rule_text: 
	flavor_text: <i-flavor></i-flavor>
	loyalty_cost_1: 
	loyalty_cost_2: +1
	loyalty_cost_3: -3
	loyalty_cost_4: -8
	loyalty_cost_5: 
	loyalty: 4
	level_1_text: When Kathleen, Frog Queen enters, you <kw-1><nospellcheck><key>become the monarch</key></nospellcheck>.<atom-reminder-action> <i-auto>(The monarch draws a card at the beginning of their end step. Whenever the monarch is dealt combat damage by a creature, its controller becomes the monarch.)</i-auto></atom-reminder-action></kw-1>
	level_2_text: <margin:130:0:0>Create a 1/1 Frog token.</margin:130:0:0>
	level_3_text: <margin:130:0:0>Target creature or artifact transforms into a 1/1 Frog with no abilities.</margin:130:0:0>
	level_4_text: <margin:130:0:0>If you are the monarch, you control target opponent during their next turn.</margin:130:0:0>
	level_5_text: 
	card_code_text: 
	card_code_text_2: 
	card_code_text_3: 
	copyright: 
	copyright_2: 
	copyright_3: 
	special_text:
		When Kathleen, Frog Queen enters, you <kw-1><nospellcheck><key>become the monarch</key></nospellcheck>.<atom-reminder-action> <i-auto>(The monarch draws a card at the beginning of their end step. Whenever the monarch is dealt combat damage by a creature, its controller becomes the monarch.)</i-auto></atom-reminder-action></kw-1>
		[+1]: <margin:130:0:0>Create a 1/1 Frog token.</margin:130:0:0>
		[-3]: <margin:130:0:0>Target creature or artifact transforms into a 1/1 Frog with no abilities.</margin:130:0:0>
		[-8]: <margin:130:0:0>If you are the monarch, you control target opponent during their next turn.</margin:130:0:0>

It will usually have this stylesheet - "stylesheet: m15-mainframe-planeswalker" -, but you can 100% confirm it's a Planeswalker by its supertype - "super_type: <word-list-type-en>Legendary Planeswalker</word-list-type-en>". So the supertype should be primary way to idenfity planeswalkers (I use stylesheet that have "planeswalker" in the name for other stuff). 

It's going to be tricky, as planeswalkers have completely different features from cards. They need the usual name, design type, rarity, alias, notes etc, but they also have a special "loyalty: 4" that should correspond to an attribute "Starting Loyalty" that only planeswalkers should have.

As for its abilities, they are not defined by the "rule_text:" field in the input, but by "loyalty_cost_1:" type fields and "level_1_text:". Planeswalkers have multiple abilities, each having a loyalty cost, and a level ability text. They correspond one by one, so for example, for Kathleen:
- "loyalty_cost_1: " and "level_1_text: When Kathleen, Frog Queen enters, you <kw-1><nospellcheck><key>become the monarch</key></nospellcheck>.<atom-reminder-action> <i-auto>(The monarch draws a card at the beginning of their end step. Whenever the monarch is dealt combat damage by a creature, its controller becomes the monarch.)</i-auto></atom-reminder-action></kw-1>" correspond together.
- "loyalty_cost_2: +1" corresponds to "level_2_text: <margin:130:0:0>Create a 1/1 Frog token.</margin:130:0:0>"
- etc.
- "loyalty_cost_5: " corresponds to "level_5_text: ". Note, this is basically an empty ability, and should be discarded if both cost and level fields are empty

I see the "special_text" field has exactly the rule text in the format I want (I didn't know this field existed until now)
"special_text:
		When Kathleen, Frog Queen enters, you <kw-1><nospellcheck><key>become the monarch</key></nospellcheck>.<atom-reminder-action> <i-auto>(The monarch draws a card at the beginning of their end step. Whenever the monarch is dealt combat damage by a creature, its controller becomes the monarch.)</i-auto></atom-reminder-action></kw-1>
		[+1]: <margin:130:0:0>Create a 1/1 Frog token.</margin:130:0:0>
		[-3]: <margin:130:0:0>Target creature or artifact transforms into a 1/1 Frog with no abilities.</margin:130:0:0>
		[-8]: <margin:130:0:0>If you are the monarch, you control target opponent during their next turn.</margin:130:0:0>"

I want this field basically to make up the rules text attribute for planeswalkers. Of course, strip unnecessary tags, especially the <margin> tags. But also take note, I'm not sure if this is going to be 100% ok, and at some point we may pivot to using the actual loyalty_cost/level_text combo. But for now, use the "special_text" field. Please also just format the text as you would any card (for tags, keywords & reminders etc.)

Please note, that I don't want Planeswalker parsing to trigger reviews solely because the "stylesheet: m15-mainframe-planeswalker" is not supported by that list. I will say now, if the planeswalker doesn't have an alias or has an empty alias field, it should auto-parse with a "Normal" design type, otherwise if it has one it should trigger the modal as the alias may indicate a special version (also ignore this if it's an Evolution Planeswalker, as an Evolution Planeswalker will always have an Alias so it doesn't indicate anything).

2. Emblem parsing

An example:
card:
	stylesheet: m15-emblem-name-cut
	stylesheet_version: 2024-06-06
	has_styling: false
	notes: 
	time_created: 2024-04-02 20:57:47
	time_modified: 2025-10-14 20:54:12
	extra_data:
		magic-counter-m15: counter_symbol: 
	name: Scorched Earth Emblem
	image: 
	image_2: 
	mainframe_image: 
	mainframe_image_2: 
	super_type: <word-list-type-en>Emblem</word-list-type-en>
	super_type_2: <word-list-type-en></word-list-type-en>
	super_type_3: <word-list-type-en></word-list-type-en>
	super_type_4: <word-list-type-en></word-list-type-en>
	sub_type: <word-list-planeswalker>Solar Flare</word-list-planeswalker>
	sub_type_2: 
	sub_type_3: 
	sub_type_4: 
	rarity: special
	rule_text: Whenever a creature you don’t control attacks or blocks, put a -1/-1 counter on it.
	flavor_text: <i-flavor></i-flavor>
	card_code_text: 
	card_code_text_2: 
	card_code_text_3: 
	copyright: 
	copyright_2: 
	copyright_3: 

Again, emblems have some particularities. They have the "super_type: <word-list-type-en>Emblem</word-list-type-en>" supertype.
Emblems are actually similar to cards, it's jsut that the "subtype" like "sub_type: <word-list-planeswalker>Solar Flare</word-list-planeswalker>" usually refers to another card (a planeswalker), so that card should be in the Related cards without me needing to have a "Related Cards" list in the notes necessarily. Also, Emblem's rarity will probably always be special, so it shouldn't trigger the modal for special rarity detected. Emblems should also be auto-sent to the tokens DB (I'm not sure at this point if Token DB has a design type attribute, but if it has, Emblems are always "Normal" i guess). Emblems also don't have a cost, so cost shouldn't be a mandatory attribute if it is.

Please note, that I don't want Emblem parsing to trigger reviews solely because the "stylesheet: m15-emblem-name-cut" is not supported by that list or that they detect rarity as special. I think it's safe to always assume anything with "stylesheet: m15-emblem-name-cut" should be handled as an Emblem, but double check the typing to be safe. If they don't coincide, then trigger a review.

3. Leyline parsing

card:
	stylesheet: future-planeswalker-horizontal
	stylesheet_version: 2024-05-29
	has_styling: true
	styling_data:
		text_box_mana_symbols: magic-mana-small.mse-symbol-font
		center_text: always
		overlay: 
	notes: 
	time_created: 2025-01-08 03:22:48
	time_modified: 2026-04-20 22:56:11
	extra_data:
		magic-m15-meld: meld_image: 
		magic-m15-meld-3in1: meld_image: 
		magic-new-planeswalker-pokemon: evocon: 
	name: Leyline of Equilibrium
	casting_cost: 3WW
	image: 
	image_2: 
	mainframe_image: 
	mainframe_image_2: 
	super_type: <word-list-type-en>Enchantment</word-list-type-en>
	sub_type: <word-list-enchantment>Leyline</word-list-enchantment><soft><atom-sep> </atom-sep></soft><word-list-enchantment></word-list-enchantment>
	rarity: rare
	rule_text:
		If Leyline of Equilibrium is in your opening hand, you may begin the game with it on the battlefield.
		<i>Years of power scaling have broken down the core concepts of this everchanging reality.</i>
		When Leyline of Equilibrium enters, each player gains 10 life. Each player’s starting life total becomes 30.
	flavor_text: <i-flavor></i-flavor>
	loyalty: 
	illustrator: 
	card_code_text: 
	card_code_text_2: 
	card_code_text_3: 
	copyright: 
	copyright_2: 
	copyright_3: 

Again, Leylines are detected specifically by the "Leyline" subtype "sub_type: <word-list-enchantment>Leyline</word-list-enchantment><soft><atom-sep> </atom-sep></soft><word-list-enchantment></word-list-enchantment>". Note that this is an enchantment subtype though, so be careful to make sure that the subtype is verified. The main chance is that it uses "stylesheet: future-planeswalker-horizontal" stylesheet, so that shouldn't trigger reviews solely if they find it anymore, and that the flavour text is interposed inbetween actual ability rules text:

	rule_text:
		If Leyline of Equilibrium is in your opening hand, you may begin the game with it on the battlefield.
		<i>Years of power scaling have broken down the core concepts of this everchanging reality.</i>
		When Leyline of Equilibrium enters, each player gains 10 life. Each player’s starting life total becomes 30.

the text between the <i></i> tags (and no other tags aka isn't keyword or keyword reminder text) is flavor text, and should be stored with flavor text. 

Please note, that I don't want Leyline parsing to trigger reviews solely because the "stylesheet: future-planeswalker-horizontal" is not supported by that list. I think it's safe to always assume anything with "stylesheet: future-planeswalker-horizontal" should be handled as an Emblem, but double check the typing to be safe. If they don't coincide, then trigger a review. Also, for the design type, "Normal" should be fine.

4. Saga parsing

card:
	stylesheet: m15-saga
	stylesheet_version: 2024-09-23
	has_styling: true
	styling_data:
		chapter_textboxes: three
		other_options: universes beyond
		casting_cost_mana_symbols: magic-mana-large.mse-symbol-font
		text_box_mana_symbols: magic-mana-small.mse-symbol-font
		overlay: 
	notes: 
	time_created: 2024-06-04 11:02:05
	time_modified: 2026-02-15 19:20:50
	card_color: green
	name: Three Visits
	casting_cost: 2GG
	image: 
	image_2: 
	mainframe_image: 
	mainframe_image_2: 
	super_type: <word-list-type-en>Enchantment</word-list-type-en>
	super_type_2: <word-list-type-en></word-list-type-en>
	super_type_3: <word-list-type-en></word-list-type-en>
	super_type_4: <word-list-type-en></word-list-type-en>
	sub_type: <word-list-enchantment>Saga</word-list-enchantment><soft><atom-sep> </atom-sep></soft><word-list-enchantment></word-list-enchantment>
	sub_type_2: 
	sub_type_3: 
	sub_type_4: 
	rarity: rare
	chapter_text: <kw-A><nospellcheck><key>Read ahead</key></nospellcheck><atom-reminder-expert> <i-auto>(Choose a chapter and start with that many lore counters. Add one after your draw step. Skipped chapters don’t trigger. Sacrifice after III.)</i-auto></atom-reminder-expert></kw-A>
	rule_text: 
	flavor_text: <i-flavor></i-flavor>
	level_1_text: Search your library for a Forest card, put it in your hand, then shuffle.
	level_2_text: Search your library for a basic land card, put it in your hand, then shuffle.
	level_3_text: Search your library for a basic land, put it on the battlefield tapped, then shuffle.
	card_code_text: 
	card_code_text_2: 
	card_code_text_3: 
	copyright: 
	copyright_2: 
	copyright_3: 
	special_text:
		<kw-A><nospellcheck><key>Read ahead</key></nospellcheck><atom-reminder-expert> <i-auto>(Choose a chapter and start with that many lore counters. Add one after your draw step. Skipped chapters don’t trigger. Sacrifice after III.)</i-auto></atom-reminder-expert></kw-A>
		I — Search your library for a Forest card, put it in your hand, then shuffle.
		II — Search your library for a basic land card, put it in your hand, then shuffle.
		III — Search your library for a basic land, put it on the battlefield tapped, then shuffle.

Similar to others, sagas use a stylesheet not handled until now "stylesheet: m15-saga" that trigger reviews.
Like Leylines, a saga is determined by the enchantment's subtype of "sub_type: <word-list-enchantment>Saga</word-list-enchantment><soft><atom-sep> </atom-sep></soft><word-list-enchantment></word-list-enchantment>"

Like Planeswalkers, sagas use separate "level_1_text" fields and a "chapter_text:" field, but the "special_text" field seems to encapsulate the entire format I want. So use that as the card's "Rule text", but note that, like Leylines, and <i>...</i> text without other tags is probably flavor text, and should be put in the flavor text, one entry under another if they are on different lines.
e.g.: 
I - You gain 4 life. <i>“Deep into the Arreat crater...”</i>
II - Draw a card. <i> “...lies the smallest door...” </i>
The flavor should be put one under another in the flavor field, with linebreaks:
“Deep into the Arreat crater...”
“...lies the smallest door...”
Also, note that there is an exception. Apart from the Read Ahead reminder text (which has keyword tags anyway), text like this: "(As this Saga enters and after your draw step, add a lore counter. Sacrifice after __X__.)" with __X__ being a variable word is never flavor text, and should be kept as ability text, despite being in italics with possibly no other tags. In fact, assume that the first line/section of each saga is never flavor text, and the flavor text only corresponds to the abilities (marked with I - , II - etc.)

Please note, that I don't want Saga parsing to trigger reviews solely because the "stylesheet: m15-saga" is not supported by that list. I think it's safe to always assume anything with "stylesheet: m15-saga" should be handled as a Saga, but double check the typing to be safe. If they don't coincide, then trigger a review. Also, for the design type, "Normal" should be fine.

Anyways, these are sensitive changes, so this is all for this wave